Courses in an University

💡 Approach

For every course i, A[i] tells how many prerequisites it needs.

The minimum number of courses that must be prerequisites for some course is:

max(A[i])

So the maximum number of courses that are not prerequisites is:

Answer = N - max(A[i])

🔑 Key Observation

We don’t need to construct the prerequisite graph.

Just find the maximum value in A.

⏱️ Complexity

* Time: O(N)
* Space: O(N)

💻 Code

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    int T;
    cin >> T;
    while (T--) {
        int N;
        cin >> N;
        vector<int> A(N);
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            cin >> A[i];
        }
        int maxPrereq = *max_element(A.begin(), A.end());
        cout << N - maxPrereq << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
